  "full_response": "The main types of software testing include:\n\n1. Unit Testing: This type of testing is performed to verify each individual component or function of a software application. It should be used at the beginning of the testing cycle after all the components have been developed to ensure the correct functionality at the smallest component level.\n\n\n2. Integration Testing: This testing comes after unit testing is complete. It focuses on finding errors in the interaction between integrated units of the software and involves testing entire sections at a time. They should be used to catch bugs that occur when components work together.\n\n\n3. System Testing: System testing is carried out after integration testing when entire software system runs together, but before system integration testing. This testing emphasizes the assessment of \"black box\" and looking at system holistically. It should be used when you want to validate the system's compliance with the specified requirements.\n\n\n4. Interface Testing: This testing type is used when two applications need to communicate with each other and should be used just before system testing. Interface testing ensures that different work as expected.\n\n\n5. Regression Testing: Regression testing verifies that previously developed and tested software still performs after a change or enhancement. It should be used whenever a new change is introduced or a bug is fixed.\n\n\n6. Performance Testing: This testing type is used when the aim is to analyze the product for its performance. It should be used when you want to determine how a system reacts under normal and usage.\n\n\n7. Usability Testing: This testing type aims to assess the ease-of-use and user-friendliness of a software application. It should used whenever any transaction-driven software application is developed to ensure the end-user's experience isn't compromised.\n\n\n8. Security Testing: This is another type of testing when the goal is to identify any possible vulnerabilities, breaches, and threats to the security of the software system. It should be used throughout the entire software development lifecycle when the software handles sensitive data about the users.\n\n\n9. Load Testing: Load testing performs testing to find the limitations of a given software system under a simulated, compressive workload. It's most beneficial in infrastructure or system-critical environments where your software would have to interact under heavy loads or volumes of data. Load testing should be used at least once seamlessly.",
